{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal supplies a throttling mechanism but no causal route from reduced generative capacity to retirement of existing rivals. Scaling variator μ inversely with unretired warrants can suppress new position spawning, but the observed ~60 accepted rivals survive because they are unattacked or poorly attacked; the mechanism never maps coverage debt to warrant resolution, attack expansion, or grounded-extension reclassification. Thus it can freeze the system at a high-debt plateau rather than drive it 'back to a sustainable equilibrium.' It also gives no sandboxed_python_v1 simulation for the claimed decay–equilibrium dynamics.","counterexample":["Suppose 60 unretired rivals each carry outstanding coverage debt and μ is driven near zero by the decay function. No new rivals spawn, but no new attacks resolve existing warrants, so the Dung grounded extension still accepts all 60. Rivalry is capped, but retirement remains ~none, contradicting the claimed return to sustainable equilibrium."],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The forced-choice degradation is causally underspecified. It asserts that high coverage debt lowers survival probability, but gives no rule for how coverage debt is converted into a defeat relation the Dung grounded extension will accept, which rival is chosen for retirement when multiple debt-heavy nodes compete, or how foreign-school warrants are forced to closure. Without that selection mechanism, 'forced choice' is stochastic pruning rather than argumentative retirement. The Wikipedia research proposal also names topics but gives no specific https URLs, and no evidence block ids are cited for the claims about variators and grounded extensions.","counterexample":["Two rivals A and B have equal coverage debt. The forced-choice criterion has no tie-break or priority rule, so the grounded extension cannot deterministically retire either; both survive, leaving rivalry unbounded despite the proposed degradation trigger."],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}